---
import ServiceLayout from '../../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
import { services } from '../../data/site';
const service = services.find((s) => s.slug === 'revenue-engine-build')!;

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Is this a new build or a rebuild?', a: 'Both, and the second is more common. Most companies calling us already own Salesforce and are living with a configuration that no longer matches how they sell. We keep whatever still earns its place.' },
  { q: 'How long does it take?', a: 'Three to six months for most mid-market builds, phased so you get working pieces along the way rather than one release at the end. Anything quoted at six weeks for a full revenue system is being quoted by someone who has not asked enough questions yet.' },
  { q: 'What about our data?', a: 'Migration is planned as its own workstream, not an afterthought in the final week. Field mapping is drafted, reviewed by someone who knows the business, tested against a full copy, then run. Bad migrations are the most common reason a good build fails.' },
  { q: 'Will our team actually use it?', a: 'That depends on whether they were part of designing it. We interview the people who live in the system daily, and we treat a rep quietly keeping a private spreadsheet as a design failure rather than a discipline problem.' },
  { q: 'Do you do the integrations too?', a: 'Yes. Marketing platform, billing, telephony, data warehouse and whatever internal system nobody wants to touch. Integration is usually where a build either becomes useful or becomes shelfware.' },
  { q: 'What happens when you leave?', a: 'You get the org, documented, with deployment history and a named person on your side who has been walked through it. We do not hold the keys and there is no retainer you have to sign to keep it working.' },
];
---
<ServiceLayout service={service} faqs={faqs} ctaHeading="Bring us the build you are living with.">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
      <h2>Configured for a demo, not for your business.</h2>
      <div class="grid grid--2" style="margin-top:2.4rem;">
        <div>
          <p>Most Salesforce builds get designed around the platform instead of the company. The stages come from a template. The fields come from whatever the last consultant had handy. Six months later the process on screen and the process in the room have quietly stopped being the same thing.</p>
          <p>Reps adapt. They keep the real pipeline somewhere else and fill in Salesforce on Thursday afternoon so the dashboard looks acceptable. Now you are paying for a system of record that records fiction.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>A rebuild starts from how your team already sells: who touches a deal, what has to be true before it moves, where the handoffs are, and which numbers somebody is actually judged on.</p>
          <p>Then the configuration follows that. Fewer fields, enforced where it matters, and reporting that means the same thing to sales, finance and the board.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">What you get</p>
      <h2>The whole system, not just the CRM.</h2>
      <div class="grid grid--2" style="margin-top:2.4rem;">
        <ul class="ticks">
          <li><strong>A data model that fits.</strong> Objects, stages and fields designed around your process, with the fields nobody has filled in since 2023 removed rather than inherited.</li>
          <li><strong>Automation that holds.</strong> Flows and Apex built to a consistent pattern, version controlled, with test coverage that means something when the next person changes it.</li>
          <li><strong>Integrations.</strong> Marketing platform, billing, telephony and warehouse wired in properly, with error handling that tells somebody when a sync fails.</li>
        </ul>
        <ul class="ticks">
          <li><strong>Migration you can audit.</strong> Mapped, reviewed, dry-run against a full copy, reconciled after the fact so you can prove nothing was lost.</li>
          <li><strong>Reporting one version of.</strong> Pipeline, forecast and source attribution defined once, so the argument about whose number is right stops.</li>
          <li><strong>Deployment discipline.</strong> Sandboxes, version control and a release path, so future changes stop going straight into production on a Friday.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it runs</p>
      <h2>Phased, with something working at each step.</h2>
      <ol class="stages">
        <li><span>Phase 1</span><div><h3>Design</h3><p>We interview the people who use the system daily and write down how selling actually happens here. That document is what everything gets built against, and you sign it off before a single field is created.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>Phase 2</span><div><h3>Core build</h3><p>Data model, process and automation, built in a sandbox and demonstrated every fortnight. You see it going wrong early enough to say so, which is the entire point of showing you.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>Phase 3</span><div><h3>Integrate and migrate</h3><p>Connected systems and data brought across, dry-run first and reconciled after. This is the phase most projects underestimate, so it gets its own time rather than being squeezed into the last fortnight.</p></div></li>
        <li><span>Phase 4</span><div><h3>Adopt and hand over</h3><p>Training built around real tasks rather than a feature tour, a support window while habits change, then documentation and handover to whoever owns it next.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--ink">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Fit</p>
      <h2>Who this is not for.</h2>
      <ul class="ticks" style="margin-top:1.8rem;">
        <li>Teams under about ten Salesforce users. The overhead outweighs the benefit and something simpler will serve you better.</li>
        <li>Companies wanting a like-for-like rebuild of what they already have. If the answer is already decided, you want an implementer with a lower rate than ours.</li>
        <li>Programmes where no executive will make a decision. Design needs someone with the authority to settle disagreements between teams, and without that the project stalls at phase one no matter who builds it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceLayout>

<style>
  .stages { list-style: none; margin: 2.4rem 0 0; padding: 0; }
  .stages li { display: grid; gap: .4rem 2.5rem; padding: 1.9rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); }
  @media (min-width: 820px) { .stages li { grid-template-columns: 130px 1fr; } }
  .stages span { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .78rem; color: var(--navy); padding-top: .35rem; letter-spacing: .04em; }
  .stages h3 { margin: 0 0 .5rem; }
  .stages p { margin: 0; }
</style>
